hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:
"15476","Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj said: Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman narrated to me, from Ali al-Azdi, from Ubayd ibn Umayr, from Abd Allah ibn Hubshi al-Khathami, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: Which deeds are best? He said: Faith without doubt in it, and jihad without misappropriation in it, and an accepted hajj. It was said: Then which prayer is best? He said: Long standing. It was said: Then which charity is best? He said: The effort of the poor. It was said: Then which migration is best? He said: Whoever abandons what Allah forbade him. It was said: Then which jihad is best? He said: Whoever strives against the polytheists with his wealth and himself. It was said: Then which killing is noblest? He said: Whose blood is shed and whose horse is hamstrung."
